---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Figure from '~/components/Figure.astro';
import Assumptions from '~/components/Assumptions.astro';
import Callout from '~/components/Callout.astro';
import Math from '~/components/Math.astro';
import Badge from '~/components/Badge.astro';
import PairCutoffLab from '~/islands/PairCutoffLab';
import MadelungLab from '~/islands/MadelungLab';

const pairAssumptions = [
  { term: 'System', value: 'Na⁺ (+1 e) and Cl⁻ (−1 e) as point charges in vacuum; r from 2.4 to 14 Å.' },
  { term: 'Coulomb', value: 'E_C = k q₁q₂/r, k = e²/4πε₀ = 14.3996 eV·Å. Negative energy = attraction.' },
  { term: 'Toy short-range term', value: 'E_sr = E_C · u(r/r_c) with the DimeNet polynomial envelope, p = 6. Exactly zero for r ≥ r_c. Not the form of any real MLIP.' },
  { term: 'Not modelled', value: 'Short-range repulsion, polarisation and any environment: this is the bare electrostatic pair term.' },
];

const madelungAssumptions = [
  { term: 'System', value: 'Ideal rock-salt lattice of point charges ±1 e; nearest-neighbour distance r₀ = 2.82 Å (NaCl). Reference ion: the Na⁺ at the centre.' },
  { term: 'Quantity', value: 'Madelung constant M, dimensionless: each ion has electrostatic energy E_i = −M k e²/r₀ (≈ −8.92 eV for NaCl).' },
  { term: 'Partial sums', value: 'Spheres: all ions with |n| ≤ R. Cubes: max|nᵢ| ≤ N. Neutral cubes: Evjen weights ½, ¼, ⅛ on faces, edges, corners.' },
  { term: 'Ewald', value: 'Conventional cubic cell (4 Na⁺ + 4 Cl⁻), α = 1.2/r₀, real-space images to ±3 cells, reciprocal vectors to |m| ≤ 8. Converged to < 10⁻⁶.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Can an atom feel beyond the cutoff?"
  description="Physics Lab P1: a cutoff-limited pair term against the Coulomb interaction, and the Madelung sum of rock salt over spheres, cubes and Ewald summation — computed live in your browser."
>
  <article class="page-grid lab-page">
    <header class="wide head">
      <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/physics-lab/">Physics Lab</a> · P1</p>
      <h1 class="h-display">Can an atom feel beyond the cutoff?</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Most machine-learned potentials build the energy from atoms inside a cutoff sphere a few ångström wide. Charges
        do not respect that sphere. Two experiments show what a cutoff misses, and why even summing “everything” is
        harder than it sounds.
      </p>
      <div class="head-badges">
        <Badge label="toy-model" />
        <Badge label="live-calculation" />
        <Badge label="theory" />
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="block" id="pair" aria-labelledby="pair-h">
      <h2 id="pair-h" class="h-section">1 · Two ions and a cutoff</h2>
      <p>
        Two point charges interact through Coulomb’s law. The energy falls off only as 1/<i>r</i>:
      </p>
      <Math display number="1" tex={String.raw`\sym{EC}{E_{\mathrm C}}(\sym{r}{r}) = \frac{k\,q_1 q_2}{\sym{r}{r}}, \qquad k = \frac{e^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0} = 14.3996\ \mathrm{eV}\,\text{Å}`} />
      <p>
        Now build the crudest possible local model: keep the Coulomb pair term, but switch it off smoothly at the cutoff
        <Math tex={String.raw`\sym{rc}{r_{\mathrm c}}`} /> with the same polynomial envelope <i>u</i> used for MLIP radial bases
        (<a href="/method/design-system/#fig-cutoff">see the cutoff explorer</a>):
      </p>
      <Math display number="2" tex={String.raw`\sym{Esr}{E_{\mathrm{sr}}}(\sym{r}{r}) = \sym{EC}{E_{\mathrm C}}(\sym{r}{r})\; u\!\left(\sym{r}{r}/\sym{rc}{r_{\mathrm c}}\right), \qquad \sym{Esr}{E_{\mathrm{sr}}} = 0 \ \text{ for } \sym{r}{r} \ge \sym{rc}{r_{\mathrm c}}`} />
      <p>
        Drag the ions apart. Inside the cutoff the toy term tracks Coulomb’s law; past <i>r</i><sub>c</sub> it is exactly
        zero, while the true attraction is still more than an electronvolt at 10 Å.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="wide fig-wrap">
      <Figure title="A Coulomb pair against a cutoff-limited pair term" labels={['toy-model']} number={1} id="fig-pair" framed={false}>
        <PairCutoffLab client:visible />
        <Fragment slot="caption">
          The blue curve is the Coulomb energy (1); the dashed amber curve is the toy short-range term (2). The shaded band is
          what the toy misses. Past the cutoff the band <em>is</em> the interaction: any pair term that vanishes at
          <i>r</i><sub>c</sub> misses it entirely, however it is shaped inside.
        </Fragment>
        <Assumptions slot="assumptions" items={pairAssumptions} />
        <Fragment slot="source">Definitions on this page; envelope from J. Gasteiger, J. Groß, S. Günnemann, DimeNet, ICLR 2020 (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.03123">arXiv:2003.03123</a>).</Fragment>
      </Figure>
    </div>

    <section class="block">
      <Callout kind="caveat" title="What this toy is not">
        Real MLIPs do not learn a damped Coulomb term. Their short-range energy is a learned function of the whole
        neighbourhood, and stacked message-passing layers let information travel several cutoffs (see
        <a href="/#fig-1">Fig. 1 on the homepage</a>). What survives is the conclusion: an interaction between two atoms
        farther apart than the receptive field is invisible to a purely local model.
      </Callout>
    </section>

    <section class="block" id="madelung" aria-labelledby="mad-h">
      <h2 id="mad-h" class="h-section">2 · A crystal: the sum that refuses to converge</h2>
      <p>
        If one cutoff is too short, why not sum over every ion? In rock salt each ion sees alternating shells of opposite
        charge, and its electrostatic energy is set by the Madelung constant
      </p>
      <Math display number="3" tex={String.raw`E_i = -\frac{M\,k e^2}{r_0}, \qquad M = -\sum_{\mathbf n \neq \mathbf 0} \frac{(-1)^{n_1+n_2+n_3}}{|\mathbf n|}`} />
      <p>
        where <b>n</b> runs over the integer lattice in units of the nearest-neighbour distance <i>r</i><sub>0</sub>. The
        series looks innocent but converges only <em>conditionally</em>: its value depends on the order in which you add the
        terms. Grow a sphere around the central ion and the partial sums never settle; grow a cube and they do. Make each
        cube charge-neutral with fractional boundary weights (Evjen, 1932) and they converge within a few shells.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="wide fig-wrap">
      <Figure title="Partial Madelung sums of rock salt over spheres, cubes and neutral cubes" labels={['live-calculation', 'theory']} number={2} id="fig-madelung" framed={false}>
        <MadelungLab client:visible />
        <Fragment slot="caption">
          Left: the crystal, with ions inside the summation region in full colour (amber tint = fractional Evjen weight).
          Right: the running estimate of <i>M</i> as the region grows, computed in your browser, against the Ewald result
          (green line). The sphere sums swing by more than ±5 because a sphere boundary encloses a net charge; that surplus,
          not distant detail, dominates. The plot continues to 12 <i>r</i><sub>0</sub>; the 3D view stops at 5.
        </Fragment>
        <Assumptions slot="assumptions" items={madelungAssumptions} />
        <Fragment slot="source">
          Divergence of sphere sums and convergence of cube sums: D. Borwein, J. M. Borwein, K. F. Taylor, J. Math. Phys. 26,
          2999 (1985). Neutral cubes: H. M. Evjen, Phys. Rev. 39, 675 (1932). Method: P. P. Ewald, Ann. Phys. 369, 253 (1921).
        </Fragment>
      </Figure>
    </div>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="ewald-h">
      <h2 id="ewald-h" class="h-section">Ewald’s way out</h2>
      <p>
        Ewald summation splits every 1/<i>r</i> into a part that dies quickly in real space and a smooth part that dies
        quickly in reciprocal space:
      </p>
      <Math display number="4" tex={String.raw`\frac{1}{r} = \underbrace{\frac{\operatorname{erfc}(\alpha r)}{r}}_{\text{short range: real space}} + \underbrace{\frac{\operatorname{erf}(\alpha r)}{r}}_{\text{smooth: reciprocal space}}`} />
      <p>Both pieces converge absolutely, so the order of summation no longer matters. For the potential at ion <i>i</i> in a periodic cell of volume <i>V</i>:</p>
      <Math display number="5" tex={String.raw`\varphi_i = \sum_{j}\sum_{\mathbf L}{}^{'} q_j \frac{\operatorname{erfc}(\alpha |\mathbf r_{ij}+\mathbf L|)}{|\mathbf r_{ij}+\mathbf L|} + \frac{4\pi}{V}\sum_{\mathbf k\neq 0}\frac{e^{-k^2/4\alpha^2}}{k^2}\sum_j q_j \cos(\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf r_{ij}) - \frac{2\alpha}{\sqrt\pi}\,q_i`} />
      <p>
        The figure runs exactly this, with α = 1.2/<i>r</i><sub>0</sub>, and recovers <i>M</i> = 1.747565 to six digits.
        Changing α moves weight between the two sums but not the answer, which the Atlas test suite checks.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="mlip-h">
      <h2 id="mlip-h" class="h-section">What this means for machine-learned potentials</h2>
      <ul class="takeaways">
        <li>
          <strong>A cutoff is a modelling choice, not physics.</strong> Electrostatics between charged or polar fragments
          reaches far beyond typical cutoffs of 4–6 Å, and further than a few message-passing layers in many systems.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>“Just sum everything” is not well defined for periodic systems.</strong> Long-range models therefore
          predict charge-like quantities locally and hand them to an Ewald-type sum, as in latent Ewald summation
          (B. Cheng, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41524-025-01577-7">npj Comput. Mater. 2025</a>), or solve for charges
          self-consistently (charge equilibration).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Which systems need it is an empirical question.</strong> A 2026 perspective sorts the approaches into local
          charge models, deliberately non-local models and finite-field coupling (Grasselli et al.,
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.11071">arXiv:2602.11071</a>). Interfaces with ions and polar solvents, like the
          one on the <a href="/">homepage</a>, are where the question is sharpest.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <Callout kind="note" title="Next in the Physics Lab">
        P2 grows the receptive field layer by layer (start with <a href="/">Fig. 1</a>), and the electrochemistry lab
        returns to these sums with water dipoles at a metal surface.
      </Callout>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

